with q as (
  select $l20_quiz$[{"id": "q1", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "After six months in orbit, elite astronauts often cannot stand on return and must be carried from the capsule. The chapter argues this is not a malfunction. What is it?", "options": ["Simple muscle fatigue from the cramped capsule", "The body working correctly: it continuously adapts to its load, and in weightlessness it correctly shed every gravity-fighting system (weight-bearing bone, anti-gravity muscle, the uphill-pumping heart), leaving it perfectly adapted to the wrong world", "Radiation damage to the nervous system", "Dehydration from the return journey"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The body is not adapted to Earth so much as it continuously computes an adaptation to whatever load it feels \u2014 maintain what is loaded, shed what is not. On Earth that rule is a feature (bones and muscles track demand). In freefall the skeleton, the anti-gravity muscles, and the gravity-fighting heart are all suddenly unloaded, so the body efficiently reclaims them. The astronaut who cannot stand is not broken; she is exquisitely adapted to weightlessness and therefore unfit for the gravity she came home to. Gravity was never just a burden \u2014 it was a constant signal that kept every system calibrated."}, {"id": "q2", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Why does weightlessness dissolve bone so fast, and how fast is it?", "options": ["Cosmic radiation burns away the calcium; ~0.1% per year", "By Wolff's law: unloaded weight-bearing bone loses its 'maintain me' load signal, so osteoclasts win and it demineralises at ~1-1.5% per month \u2014 ten to twenty times faster than the worst earthly osteoporosis", "Lack of sunlight stops vitamin D; a few percent per decade", "The cold of space makes bone brittle; it does not actually dissolve"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Bone is a market where mechanical load is the price signal. Osteoblasts build, osteoclasts dissolve; load tips the balance toward building, unloading toward dissolving (Wolff's law). In freefall the weight-bearing bones (spine, hip, legs) are unloaded, so the body reads them as unneeded and reclaims them at ~1-1.5% a month \u2014 a postmenopausal year compressed into thirty days, 10-20x earthly osteoporosis, reaching ~30-45% over an uncorrected Mars round trip. The freed calcium risks kidney stones far from any hospital, and much of the loss is permanent."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "In freefall ~2 litres of fluid shift headward. Why does the body then throw away 10-15% of its blood volume?", "options": ["The fluid leaks out through the skin in vacuum", "Its pressure sensors (baroreceptors) in the chest and neck misread the sudden fullness of the core as a dangerous volume OVERLOAD, so the kidneys dump fluid and red-cell production falls \u2014 shedding blood volume the body will desperately need on return", "The heart pumps the extra fluid into space", "Bone loss replaces blood with calcium"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "On Earth the circulation is built to pump fluid uphill against gravity's downward drain. Remove gravity and ~1.5-2 L floods the head (the puffy face and bird legs), and the baroreceptors read the core's fullness as too much blood. Responding to the false alarm, the kidneys dump fluid and red-cell production drops, so blood volume falls 10-15% within days. That discarded volume, plus a heart that deconditions and rounds without gravity to pump against, is why raised upright on return the astronaut's blood pools in the legs and the brain is starved \u2014 orthostatic intolerance."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "What is SANS, and why is it a top-ranked risk for a Mars mission specifically?", "options": ["Space Adaptation Nausea Syndrome \u2014 motion sickness that fades in days", "Spaceflight-Associated Neuro-ocular Syndrome: the headward fluid shift raises intracranial pressure, which flattens the back of the eyeball and shifts vision in ~70% of long crews \u2014 and it may be permanent, is poorly understood, and has no reliable countermeasure, with no early return possible from Mars", "A skin condition from recycled air", "A temporary loss of colour vision that always reverses"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "SANS traces directly to the fluid shift: some of the fluid driven headward raises the pressure around the brain and optic nerves, which presses on the back of the eye and, over months, flattens the globe, swells the optic nerve head, and folds the retina \u2014 shifting vision (often toward farsightedness). It affects on the order of 70% of long-duration crews. It is a top Mars risk because it may not fully reverse, its mechanism is poorly understood with no reliable countermeasure, and \u2014 unlike on the ISS \u2014 a crew member whose sight degraded over a multi-year flight could not simply come home."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Spinning a spacecraft supplies artificial gravity via a = \u03c9\u00b2r \u2014 the one countermeasure that would fix everything at once. Why has none ever been flown?", "options": ["Spinning would fling the crew off the spacecraft", "The inner ear tolerates only ~2 rpm before Coriolis forces cause sickness, and at 2 rpm a full g needs a ~224 m radius (a structure nearly half a km across); shrink it to a comfortable 10 m and it must spin at ~9.5 rpm, deep in the sickness zone \u2014 comfortable-but-enormous or compact-but-nauseating, with no cheap middle", "Rotation would interfere with the solar panels", "Artificial gravity only works near a planet"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Because a = \u03c9\u00b2r, hitting 1 g means trading spin rate against radius. But the inner ear can only take a slow spin \u2014 above ~2 rpm the Coriolis force on a turning head brings back the very nausea you were trying to cure. At 2 rpm, 1 g needs r = g/\u03c9\u00b2 \u2248 224 m, a rotating structure beyond anything ever launched. Shrink to 10 m and you must spin at ~9.5 rpm, deep in the sickness zone. That trap \u2014 comfortable but colossal, or compact but sickening \u2014 plus the cost and complexity of the compromises (partial g, intermittent spin, tethered modules) is why every long-duration crew has instead simply endured weightlessness."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "State the single logic that drives bone loss, muscle wasting, and heart deconditioning, and explain why the chapter says 'gravity is a required signal, not just a force.' Trace the logic through all three systems, and explain why muscle loss makes bone loss worse.", "rubric": "THE SINGLE LOGIC: the body maintains what is loaded and sheds what is not \u2014 it continuously computes an adaptation to the load it feels, and in weightlessness it correctly concludes that every gravity-fighting system is unneeded and reclaims it. GRAVITY AS A REQUIRED SIGNAL: four billion years of evolution in one g tuned the body AROUND gravity's constant presence, so gravity is not merely a force the body endures but a load-bearing input that keeps the skeleton, muscles, heart, fluids, and balance calibrated; remove it and nothing relaxes into ease \u2014 everything decays, because it was built around the signal being there. THROUGH THREE SYSTEMS: (1) BONE \u2014 the weight-bearing skeleton, unloaded in freefall, loses its 'maintain me' signal, so by Wolff's law osteoclasts win and it dissolves at ~1-1.5%/month (10-20x osteoporosis, ~30-45% over an uncorrected Mars trip), the freed calcium risking kidney stones and much of it lost permanently. (2) MUSCLE \u2014 the anti-gravity muscles (calves, quads, back, core) whose daily job is holding the body upright suddenly have nothing to hold up, so they atrophy ~20% in two weeks ('use it or lose it'). (3) HEART \u2014 the heart is a muscle that on Earth pumps blood uphill against gravity; in freefall that work largely disappears, so it deconditions, losing ~9-12% of its mass and even rounding from an ellipse toward a sphere. WHY MUSCLE LOSS WORSENS BONE LOSS: much of the load a bone feels comes not from body weight but from the pull of muscle on bone, so as muscles waste and pull less, the bones lose yet another loading signal and demineralise faster \u2014 a reinforcing downward spiral, both driven by the one missing gravitational load. A strong answer stresses that in every case the body is being efficient and correct, and in every case the result is a body optimised for weightlessness and therefore dangerously unfit for the gravity it must face again."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "The chapter contrasts 'solved engineering problems' with 'open biological problems' and asks whether the body is the wall for a crewed Mars mission. Explain that contrast, why the countermeasures only slow the decay rather than cure it, and what remains genuinely unknown.", "rubric": "THE CONTRAST: the EXTERNAL threats of space \u2014 vacuum, cold, no air \u2014 are, in engineering terms, SOLVED: we know how to build a pressurised, oxygenated, climate-controlled vessel (a portable fragment of Earth). The INTERNAL degradation of the body from missing gravity is NOT solved and may not be solvable with current means: you can make the environment perfect and the body STILL decays, because the one input it needs that you cannot pack into a spacecraft is the constant gravitational load it evolved around. So the environmental challenges are engineering problems with known solutions, while the physiological challenge of missing gravity is an open biological problem that may set a hard limit on how far and how long humans can go. WHY COUNTERMEASURES ONLY SLOW IT: the main countermeasure, ~2.5 hours of daily resistance/impact exercise, artificially reloads the bones and muscles and substantially slows their loss (modern crews fare better than early cosmonauts) \u2014 but it only SLOWS the loss, never stops it (the body still degrades), and it does NOTHING for the fluid shift, the deconditioning heart, or SANS; it is a hostage negotiation, not a rescue, because it treats the symptoms of a missing signal rather than restoring the signal. The one true cure \u2014 artificial gravity from a rotating spacecraft (a = \u03c9\u00b2r) \u2014 would fix everything at once by restoring the load, but comfortable rotation (~2 rpm) needs a ~224 m radius (an unbuildable structure), and a compact station must spin fast enough to sicken the crew, so none has ever flown. WHAT REMAINS UNKNOWN: whether a body can endure the years of a Mars mission and still function on arrival; whether Mars's 0.38 g partial gravity is enough to keep the body tuned (no human has ever felt sustained partial gravity); the long-term course of SANS and the fluid shift over years; and, with only thin evidence (e.g. the Kelly twin study \u2014 one person, 340 days, in low Earth orbit, most changes reversible but not all), how deep-space multi-year missions would affect the body. A strong answer connects to the through-line \u2014 never send a human where a machine would do unless what the human brings is worth the price \u2014 adding the cost L19 hadn't counted: the human payload degrades in transit, arriving diminished where a robot arrives unchanged, so the changing body may, more than any rocket-equation sum, decide whether humans can go at all."}]$l20_quiz$::jsonb as questions
